WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.595
Post-quantum cryptography prepares systems for a future where certain current public-key

00:00:04.595 --> 00:00:07.160
algorithms may no longer provide adequate protection.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.785
It does not require defenders to run quantum computers,

00:00:09.785 --> 00:00:12.120
and it is not the same as quantum key distribution.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.710
It is a standards-aware migration effort for ordinary systems that depend on key

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:16.840
establishment,

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.690
digital signatures, certificates, software signing, remote access, identity,

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:22.280
and trusted communication.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.200
The most important planning move is to understand cryptographic roles.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.560
A key encapsulation mechanism helps establish shared secrets.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.920
A digital signature supports authentication and integrity.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.800
Symmetric encryption and hashing have different risk profiles than public-key mechanisms.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:36.830
Once roles are clear,

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.930
teams can build an inventory that records where cryptography is used, what depends on it,

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.315
what data it protects, who owns it, and how migration-ready it is.

00:00:44.315 --> 00:00:48.920
A strong migration program prioritizes risk, uses vetted standards and mature libraries,

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.865
tests interoperability, coordinates with vendors, and builds crypto agility.

00:00:52.865 --> 00:00:56.790
It avoids custom cryptography and avoids unsupported combinations that cannot be

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.555
maintained.

00:00:57.555 --> 00:01:02.020
It also recognizes that engineering details matter: size, performance, certificates,

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.335
protocols, hardware, logging, monitoring,

00:01:04.335 --> 00:01:07.200
and operational support can all shape the migration path.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.040
The goal is not to predict the exact date of a quantum threat.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.435
The goal is to reduce future migration risk before urgent replacement becomes necessary.

00:01:14.435 --> 00:01:16.685
Teams that start with inventory, governance, pilots,

00:01:16.685 --> 00:01:20.525
and practical communication will be better prepared than teams that wait for a deadline.

00:01:20.525 --> 00:01:24.755
PQC is ultimately a resilience project: know what you depend on, make change possible,

00:01:24.755 --> 00:01:27.760
and move deliberately toward standards-based protection.
